 "id": "2026-11-04-ukraine-power-grid-explained",
 "format": "long",
 "lang": "en",
 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "On Tuesday, taps ran dry in parts of Kyiv after Russian strikes hit the power grid. Why do power plants keep becoming targets?"
  },
  {
   "id": "promise",
   "text": "How much of Ukraine's electricity is already gone? Why is energy infrastructure a target for both sides? Why have energy pauses not held so far? And how would a new one be checked?"
  },
  {
   "id": "ch1",
   "text": "Start with one number. Ukraine's electricity demand peaks at about twelve gigawatts, according to the International Energy Agency. Then the invasion came. Ukraine's grid is run by a state company, Ukrenergo. Its job is to balance supply and demand every second. When plants or lines are lost, it cuts demand with scheduled outages, region by region. The agency says that over 2022 and 2023, about half of Ukraine's power generation capacity was occupied by Russian forces, destroyed or damaged. The occupation of the Zaporizhzhia nuclear plant alone removed six gigawatts from the grid. About half of the large substations, the hubs that move electricity around the country, were damaged as well. Ukrainian authorities, cited by the Congressional Research Service, put the fall in available generation at fifty-one percent between early 2022 and April 2023. If you want one of these explainers every Wednesday, subscribing is how you get it.",
   "pauseBefore": 0.4
  },
  {
   "id": "ch2",
   "text": "How fast can a grid be broken? The first large wave of strikes on the grid began in October 2022. Rolling blackouts followed, and the damage kept growing. Then came the spring of 2024. Nine gigawatts, says the I.E.A. That is how much more generation Ukraine lost in the spring of 2024. It left Ukraine with about a third of its pre-war capacity. Ukraine's grid operator, Ukrenergo, gave its own figure in May 2024: more than eight gigawatts destroyed. By that summer, supply fell about two point three gigawatts short of peak demand, and Ukrenergo covered the gap with rolling blackouts. In November 2025, Foreign Policy Analytics put the share destroyed or occupied at about two thirds of pre-war capacity, and said about three gigawatts had been restored.",
   "pauseBefore": 0.4
  },
  {
   "id": "ch3",
   "text": "So why does Russia keep hitting the grid? Russia's Defense Ministry has said it strikes energy facilities that support Ukraine's military and defense industry. Ukrainian officials say the effect falls on civilians: power, heat and water cut as winter starts. The business outlet bne IntelliNews reports that Russia increasingly targets substations, not only power plants. Substations can cut power over a wide area. Ukrenergo called Tuesday's attack unprecedented in the number of weapons used, according to the Ukrainian outlet Glavnoe. The energy company DTEK said three of its thermal power plants were knocked completely offline. Large power plants in the Dnipropetrovsk, Vinnytsia and Kharkiv regions were hit. This week showed it. Quick question: how many times did Russia hit Ukrainian energy facilities in a single day? Take a guess. More than twenty times in twenty-four hours, says Ukrenergo. Emergency outages began in Kyiv and several regions, some lasting more than twelve hours. Kyiv's water utility said its main facilities lost power, and the mayor, Vitali Klitschko, said warming centers were opened. And Energy Minister Denys Shmyhal said three drones were aimed at a substation that is critical for the Khmelnytskyi nuclear plant.",
   "pauseBefore": 0.4
  },
  {
   "id": "ch4",
   "text": "Now the other side of the same war. Energy is a target in Russia too. Ukraine's president, Volodymyr Zelensky, said Ukrainian forces struck fifteen Russian oil refineries between January and May this year. Ukraine's general staff claims that by early July, about forty-three percent of Russia's refining capacity was projected to be disabled. That is a Ukrainian claim, and we found no independent confirmation. The Ukrainian outlet Liga puts the actual figure at more than thirty percent. Ukrainian analyst Mykhailo Honchar goes further and says half of Russia's primary refining capacity is offline, a claim we could not verify. Several effects have been reported. Russia has banned gasoline exports from April first, announced by Deputy Prime Minister Alexander Novak. Reuters reported in May that drone strikes forced central Russian refineries to halt or cut output. Al Jazeera reported on October eleventh that mutual attacks on energy infrastructure had put pressure on energy prices. And Bloomberg reported that Russian crude exports reached their highest level since the war began, because less oil was being refined at home.",
   "pauseBefore": 0.4
  },
  {
   "id": "ch5",
   "text": "If both sides keep losing energy, why not stop? They tried. On March eighteenth, 2025, after a phone call between Donald Trump and Vladimir Putin, the Kremlin announced a thirty-day halt on strikes against energy targets. Meduza reported that the Kremlin's list of covered facilities included oil refineries and nuclear power plants. Moscow said it began that day. Kyiv said it began a week later. Reports at the time describe accusations of violations from both sides. In a letter to the United Nations, Russia described Ukrainian strikes as violations of the moratorium. On March twenty-eighth, Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov said Moscow reserved the right not to comply. In April, the Kremlin accused Kyiv of effectively disregarding it. On April eighteenth, the Kremlin said the pause had expired. Trump had already announced an energy ceasefire in mid-September. Then, on October eleventh, Trump announced that Russia and Ukraine had agreed to an energy ceasefire. A source close to Zelensky called the announcement a surprise. In the reports we reviewed, Moscow has not confirmed an agreement. On November first and second, Zelensky said Russia hit power infrastructure in four Ukrainian regions overnight, and Russia's Defense Ministry said it downed two hundred and thirty-five Ukrainian drones, while the governor of Nizhny Novgorod reported an industrial site hit. And the Financial Times reported in September that Putin does not want a deal to halt energy strikes before winter. That is the Financial Times account, as reported by Meduza.",
   "pauseBefore": 0.4
  },
  {
   "id": "outlook",
   "text": "Talks are scheduled for November tenth and eleventh. U.S. envoy Steve Witkoff says Russia, Ukraine and the United States are scheduled to meet in Abu Dhabi. The Kremlin has confirmed it will take part, with Vladimir Medinsky as its chief negotiator. Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner are to lead the American side. Reports describe it as the first U.S.-mediated round since February. Witkoff says the agenda covers security arrangements, easing the fighting and humanitarian steps, and one report adds a possible energy and maritime ceasefire. Here is what has been reported so far. The Moscow Times notes that Putin's only apparent concession is agreeing to resume talks. And the New York Times reported on October sixteenth that Russia is planning winter strikes to cut electricity, heat and water in Kyiv, Kharkiv and Odesa. That is a reported plan, not a confirmed event. How would an energy pause be checked? In the reports we reviewed, no monitoring mechanism for a new pause is described. The March 2025 pause ended with accusations from both sides. Will Russia and Ukraine agree to stop energy strikes before January? Tell us below: yes or no.",
   "pauseBefore": 0.4
  },
  {
   "id": "next",
   "text": "Next: how sanctions on Russian oil work, what the price cap does, and what the shadow fleet is. Watch that one next.",
   "pauseBefore": 0.4
  }
 ],
