  "title": "Why Russia Keeps Hitting Ukraine's Power Grid",
  "description": "Why do power plants keep becoming targets in the war in Ukraine? This explainer shows how much of Ukraine's electricity generation has been lost, why Russia strikes substations and power plants, how Ukraine's strikes on Russian refineries fit in, how the 2025 energy-strike pause ended, and that Russia had not confirmed the October 2026 energy ceasefire announcement in the reports reviewed, and what sources say could come out of the Abu Dhabi talks on November 10 and 11. Claims are attributed to their sources; disputed or unverified claims are labelled.\n\nSources:\nInternational Energy Agency (IEA): Ukraine's Energy Security and the Coming Winter – Ukraine's energy system under attack\nIEA: Energy System Resilience – Lessons learned from Ukraine\nCongressional Research Service: Attacks on Ukraine's Electric Grid – Insights (R48067)\nForeign Policy Analytics: Investing in Energy Security – Ukraine (November 2025)\nKyiv Independent: Kyiv faces emergency power cuts, widespread water outages after Russia's largest attack on energy infrastructure this year (Nov 3, 2026)\nKyiv Post: Russia targets Ukraine's energy facilities more than 20 times in 24 hours, Ukrenergo says; Russian attack leaves Kyiv without water (Nov 3, 2026)\nEuromaidan Press: One of largest Russian attacks yet on Ukraine's energy system (Nov 4, 2026)\nbne IntelliNews: Ukraine's blackouts go round the clock as Russia increasingly targets substations\nMoscow Times / Reuters: Drone strikes force central Russian refineries to halt or cut output (May 20, 2026)\nMoscow Times / Bloomberg: Ukraine's refinery strikes push Russian crude exports to wartime high (June 2, 2026)\nUkrinform: Ukrainian forces have already struck 15 Russian oil refineries this year – Zelensky\nLiga.net: Russian refining capacity disabled by Ukrainian strikes\nMeduza: Kremlin publishes list of energy infrastructure covered under partial ceasefire (Mar 25, 2025); Russia says it will no longer observe moratorium (Apr 18, 2025)\nMoscow Times: Russia agrees to halt attacks on energy infrastructure (Mar 18, 2025)\nCNN: Trump claims Ukraine and Russia have agreed to energy ceasefire (Oct 11, 2026)\nAl Jazeera: Trump says Ukraine, Russia agree to energy ceasefire (Oct 11, 2026)\nMeduza: Financial Times reports Moscow has not agreed to any energy deal (Sep 14, 2026)\nNew York Times (via Meduza): Russia plans winter strikes on electricity, heat and water (Oct 16, 2026)\nReuters / RFE/RL / NBC News / Al Arabiya: Russian strikes on Ukrainian power infrastructure and Ukrainian drones over Russia (Nov 1–2, 2026)\nRussian Defense Ministry statements on strikes on energy facilities (as reported by wire services)\nGlavnoe / Kyiv Post: Ukrenergo and DTEK statements on the Nov 3, 2026 attack\nEuromaidan Press: Ukrainian analyst on Russian refining (Jun 30, 2026)\nAxios / CNBC / Moscow Times: U.S.-mediated Ukraine peace talks to resume in Abu Dhabi (Nov 3, 2026)\n\n#geopolitics #Ukraine #energy #explained",
